/*  The view: what the field looks like.

    It takes squares and gives back lines. It does not print them and it does not know what a key
    means — the main program prints, and the controller decides what moves. A view that printed
    would be a view you could not check by comparing its answer to an expected one.

    ★★ `spot` is simply IN SCOPE here, because `board.cufe` is in this directory and a directory
    is one namespace. There is nothing to pull and nothing to carry: the type crosses the file
    boundary the way a declaration crosses a line boundary.

    ⚠ It returns a series of lines rather than one block of text. Joining them is one line at the
    call site and splitting a block back apart is not, so the caller keeps the choice of how to
    space, indent or frame them.
*/

/// The field as one line of text per row: `O` where the snake lies, `@` for the apple.
///
/// ★ It takes the apple as a PARAMETER rather than asking where the apple is. A view that
/// fetched its own subject would decide what the frame is OF, which is the caller's business —
/// and it is what lets a test draw any position at all without arranging for one.
Bind series of text to frame-of, given (the series of spot body, the spot apple):
    Define picture as a series of text.

    For each row in range 1 to cast height, repeat:
        Define line as "".

        For each column in range 1 to cast width, repeat:
            Define here as cast at on (row, column).
            Define filled as false.

            For each piece in body, repeat:
                If cast piece's same-as on (here), the filled becomes true.
            Done.

            If filled, the line becomes line joined to "O".
            Otherwise, if cast apple's same-as on (here), the line becomes line joined to "@".
            Otherwise, the line becomes line joined to ".".
        Done.

        Insert line into picture.
    Done.

    Return picture.
Done.
